00:00:06.289 --> 00:00:06.299 align:start position:0%
competitive shooters are serious  business  they depict epic battles between  modern-day soldiers and insurgents in  war-torn cities or conflicts between  space marines and aliens on distant  worlds or skirmishes between  battle-hardened men and subterranean  creatures who try to slice each other to  bits with rifle mounted chainsaws  there's nothing wrong with any of that  but there's also no reason why the  broadly appealing mechanics of  multiplayer shooters always need to be  married to such grim scenarios that's  what makes plants vs. zombies garden  warfare so enticing mechanically it's  solid if unsurprising it distinguishes  itself from other shooters not through  its gameplay but by successfully  counting familiar shooter action in the  bright and playful world of the plants  vs. zombies games still the package  feels a bit thin even at the $40 asking  price that the game carries on the Xbox  one garden warfare is strictly a  team-based multiplayer game each mode  has you siding with either the plants or  the zombies as the age-old ERV  four-year-old conflict between them  rages on as in so many competitive  shooters gameplay is class-based there  are four basic units on each side among  the plants there's the well-rounded pea  shooter whose pea cannon does Splash  Damage and there's the sunflower whose  heal beam can give allies the extra  vitality they need to survive a shootout  among others the zombie army includes  the engineer who can call in a drone to  attack enemies from the air and rain  down explosive traffic cone-shaped death  on those pesky plants they've also got  the all-star a zombie and football getup  who's pigskin shooting cannon takes a  second to spin up but does lots of  damage once it gets going the game does  a good job of balancing out the  abilities on each side the Chomper can  burrow underground get under an enemy  and first forced to swallow him whole a  wonderfully satisfying move to pull off  but nearby zombie engineers can use  their sonic grenades to stun all nearby  enemies forcing burrowed chompers out of  the ground in the  yes and the game makes defensive  abilities just as important as offensive  ones the cactuses potato mines can  fortify a location against those zombies  who are too reckless to look where  they're going and the all-stars dummy  shields can absorb enemy fire in garden  warfare as in most of the better  competitive shooters on the market these  days you're not always focused just on  killing members of the other team you're  trying to use a variety of abilities at  your disposal can most effectively  support your team in team vanquish games  the best way to support your team is in  fact to kill members of the other team  this playlist hits teams of up to 12  against each other in a race 250 kills  and encourages you to revive fallen  teammates which subtracts the point the  opposing team earned for killing your  buddy from their tally more dynamic are  the battles of the gardens and  graveyards playlist and which plants  work together to prevent zombies from  capturing key points on the map only one  point is contested at any time so  battles for those points are heated and  if the zombies succeed in putting the  plants back to the last battleground on  a map there's an enjoyable endgame goal  that the undead path to accomplish on  one map zombies launched themselves  through a cannon to an island to assault  the mega flower and on another they have  to assault crazy Dave's mansion which is  defended by cannons that launched a  massive wall nuts that roll down the  driveway like bowling balls there's also  the cooperative garden ops game pipe in  which four players defend the garden  from waves of ai-controlled zombies if  you survive all the waves there's a left  for dead style escape attempt which some  players may survive while others don't  making for some exciting and desperate  final moments but the AI zombie boards  aren't as clever or as enjoyable to mow  down as player controlled opponents so  garden ops lacks the liveliness of the  competitive mode there are tons of  unlockables in garden warfare everything  from accessories like lipstick for your  sunflower to character variations like  the dapper agent P who sacrifices the  standard pea shooters Splash Damage for  a damage bonus on critical hits  but while the unlockable fit right in  with the whimsical spirit of the plants  versus  these franchise you're left feeling like  garden warfare needed a little something  more to make it a meal rather than a  side salad still it's that rare shooter  whose world and characters might  actually put a smile on your face making  it a refreshing entry in a genre that  usually takes itself really seriously
 

